Question 1. Yes. A 256 byte slot within a 512 byte atomicity unit means two slots share one unit. Writing one slot requires modifying the entire unit. A power loss during this write could corrupt both slots because the device performs atomic writes at the unit size, so partial writes leave the entire unit in an inconsistent state.

Question 2. Yes. If the entire 2048 byte ring fits within a single minimum_io_size unit (e.g., 64 KiB), writing any part of the ring requires writing the full unit. A power loss during such a write could corrupt the entire unit, destroying all slots in the ring and defeating the redundancy purpose.

Question 3. A device with minimum_io_size of 256 bytes would not violate the rules. Each 256 byte slot fits exactly within one minimum_io_size unit, so no sharing occurs. Writing a single slot uses one unit without affecting others.

Question 4. Slot width must equal or exceed the minimum_io_size, and slots must be spaced such that each slot resides entirely within its own minimum_io_size unit with no overlap between slots.

The assumption in Fact 2 is wrong. Atomicity width for writes should be based on minimum_io_size, not physical_block_size. Fact 5 states minimum_io_size is the relevant granularity for I/O operations, and physical_block_size may differ (e.g., devices with 512 byte physical_block_size but 4096 byte minimum_io_size). Fact 2 incorrectly ties atomicity to physical_block_size when Rule A and device behavior depend on minimum_io_size.
